            "The old stories say that whoever first tastes the salmon will have all of its knowledge, said Finnegas. I have waited seven long years to catch it.",
            "Fionn looked at the dark water and thought about all that knowledge, swimming quietly beneath the surface. Then I hope you catch it soon, he said, for you have waited so very well.",
            "Fionn helped Finnegas every day. He fetched water from the spring and gathered sticks for the fire. He swept the floor of the little hut and mended the old fishing nets. And every evening they sat together by the fire, and Finnegas told him stories.",
            "Then one bright morning, as the mist rose from the river, the old man's line gave a great tug. Finnegas leaned back and pulled, and out of the water came a beautiful salmon, its scales shining silver and pink in the sun.",
            "At last! cried Finnegas, and his eyes filled with happy tears. Seven years I have waited, and here it is.",
            "He carried the salmon up to the hut, and he gave it to Fionn. Cook this for me, my boy, he said. But listen carefully. You must not taste even the smallest bit of it. The first taste must be mine.",
            "I promise, master, said Fionn. And while Finnegas went down to the river to wash, Fionn built up the fire and set the salmon on a flat stone beside the flames to cook slowly.",
            "Fionn watched the salmon very carefully. He turned it this way and that, so it would cook evenly. The fire crackled, and the smell of supper drifted out of the little hut and over the river.",
            "As it cooked, a small bubble rose on the salmon's skin. Fionn did not want the skin to spoil, so he pressed the bubble down with his thumb. But the skin was hot, and it burned his thumb, just a little.",
            "Ouch! said Fionn, and without thinking, he popped his thumb into his mouth to cool it, just as you or I might do.",
            "And in that very moment, something wonderful happened. A warm light seemed to fill his head. All at once Fionn knew the names of every river and every hill in Ireland. He knew why the wind blows and where the swallows go in winter. He felt full of wisdom.",
            "Soon Finnegas came back from the river. He looked at Fionn's face, and he saw that something had changed. His eyes were brighter, and there was a new calm about him.",
            "Fionn, said the old man gently, have you tasted the salmon? No, master, said Fionn. But I burned my thumb on its skin, and I put my thumb in my mouth to cool it.",
            "Finnegas was quiet for a long moment. Then he smiled a wide, warm smile and laid his hand on Fionn's shoulder. Then the first taste was yours, he said. The knowledge was meant for you all along. Eat the rest of the salmon, my boy.",
            "Fionn felt a little sorry that the old man had waited so long. But Finnegas only laughed. I have spent my life learning, he said, and I have taught you all I know. That is enough for any poet.",
